// BUG 01: calling a function before its declaration statement has executed
// passes the type checker, then dies at runtime with a nameless error.
//
// Run: target\debug\rad.exe projects\dogfood\oracle\bugs\01_forward_fn_call.rad
//
// EXPECTED (per docs/src/project/adversarial-round2-findings.md line 27-29,
// "Forward references now work for top-level functions / Calling a function
// before its declaration is accepted"):  prints "twice(21) = 42".
//
// ACTUAL: compiles with zero diagnostics, then:
//     Runtime error: [line 14] in main: Not callable: nil
//
// Two distinct problems:
//   1. Static/runtime divergence. The type checker fully resolves `twice`:
//      it reports no "undefined function", and it type-checks the call site
//      (passing a str here IS reported as a compile-time type error). So the
//      declaration is in scope statically but holds nil at runtime.
//   2. The diagnostic names nothing. "Not callable: nil" omits the callee
//      name and gives no hint that declaration order is the cause.
//
// Real rule (see probes/02_forward_ref_shape.rad): `fn` declarations bind only
// when top-level execution REACHES the declaration statement, so this also
// breaks a call made from inside an earlier-declared function body.
//
// Fix should be either hoisting (match the checker and the docs) or a
// compile-time error that names the function and the ordering constraint.

let doubled = twice(21)
print(f"twice(21) = {doubled}")

fn twice(n: int) -> int {
    return n * 2
}
